module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.merge_carries_prose_true_on_one_side_and_false_on_the_other

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data merge_carries_prose_true_on_one_side_and_false_on_the_other: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "merge_carries_prose_true_on_one_side_and_false_on_the_other" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a merge brings in a prose claim that is TRUE ON THE SIDE THAT WROTE IT AND FALSE ON THE MERGED TREE, because the other side adds the behaviour the claim denies.** INVALID STATE: a doc block, annotation or receipt that correctly describes one branch, landed unmodified on a branch where it asserts the opposite of what the code does. NEITHER SIDE IS WRONG, which is why no conflict fires and why taking a side -- the only operation a text merge offers -- is exactly how the falsehood ships.",

    "**SPECIMEN, MEASURED (gunbc#11166 merging main a6ae1af643c, 2026-09-13), TWO INSTANCES IN ONE MERGE.** FIRST: main's doc on `ClaimScopeFixtureOutcome::ScopeAccepted` says acceptance is SILENT about ambiguity -- `the scope builder COLLECTS ambiguous_bare_reads and returns them on the accepted scope; it does not refuse on them` -- which is true on main and is the adjudicated wording from a side-chat review. The merging branch ADDS that refusal, so on the merged tree acceptance DOES exclude that population, and the branch's own negative controls assert through the arm that doc says establishes nothing. SECOND: main's DESIGN section 3c frontier on the same type states `the consumer it was built for is not in this diff` and names the trigger that would bring it; the merging branch IS that consumer and satisfies that trigger, so the frontier describes a state that ended. Both were resolved by rewriting against the merged tree rather than by taking either side.",

    "DISTINCT FROM `stale_claim_survives_its_own_correct_edit`, WHICH IS THE NEAREST NEIGHBOUR AND WAS CHECKED BEFORE THIS ROW WAS FILED. That class is prose that was true when written and became false through a correct edit ELSEWHERE, and its corollary is addressed to an author who is NOT looking at the citing file: `the announcement is in a diff you are not looking at`. This class inverts that. The author IS editing the file -- the merge is that edit -- and the falsehood ships anyway, because the merge presents an OURS-VERSUS-THEIRS question when the answer required is TRUE-VERSUS-FALSE-ON-THE-RESULT. Same harm, opposite ergonomics: that row loses a sentence nobody was looking at, this row loses one the author had open. A repair for that class -- searching the entailment neighbourhood after a withdrawal -- does not reach this one, because nothing was withdrawn.",

    "ALSO DISTINCT FROM `duplicate_declaration_arrives_through_a_clean_merge`, with which it shares a merge and a silence. That class is about a merge producing a state the COMPILER refuses -- loud, located, and caught by any build. This class produces a state no build can refuse, because no `Accepted` program reads an annotation (DESIGN section 4c): the tree compiles, every control passes, and the only defect is that a sentence lies.",

    "DISTINCT FROM `verdict_stale_at_the_merge_instant`, THE THIRD MERGE-FLAVOURED NEIGHBOUR, checked because a reviewer would otherwise have to ask. That row's invalid state is a VERDICT -- real, terminal and green -- computed over a tree that is not the tree the merge produces, and its staleness is a fact about a PAIR (verdict time versus merge time, verdict base versus merged base). Two things separate it from this row. Its subject is a computed RESULT and this row's subject is AUTHORED PROSE, which no `Accepted` program reads at all (DESIGN section 4c), so no rerun can refresh it. And nothing here is STALE: main's `ScopeAccepted` doc is true on main at the moment of the merge and remains true on main afterwards. The defect is not that a claim aged; it is that one text has two truth values over two trees, and the merge picks between texts rather than between truths.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: after any merge that brings in doc blocks from a branch which does NOT contain this branch's behaviour, re-read each block against WHAT THE MERGED TREE DOES, not against what either side did. The mechanical tell is a doc block adjacent to a declaration the two sides changed differently -- that adjacency is decidable even though the truth question is not. The author's tell is subtler and worth stating: reaching for `--ours` or `--theirs` on a prose hunk IS the moment, because a prose hunk has no correct side by construction when the two sides describe different behaviour.",

    "**THE LOUD VARIANT OF THE SAME MECHANISM IS STRUCTURALLY CAUGHT, AND THE CONTRAST IS THE ARGUMENT FOR DESIGN SECTION 3'S `cite the symbol, not the position`.** The same merge that produced the two prose instances above also produced a RESOLVABLE one: the branch's witness imported `claim_scope_was_refused`, a reader that existed on the branch's copy of the instrument and that gunbc#11143 DELETED as dangling before landing. Each side correct alone, the combination unresolvable -- the identical shape. But because the subject is a NAME rather than a sentence, the floor's declarations phase refused it by identity and location, `IMPORT-MEMBER-ABSENT ... which declares no such name`, instead of shipping it. So ONE merge-time mechanism is structurally caught where it lands on a citation and mitigatable-only where it lands on prose, and the whole of the difference is that a name is reachable from the namespace tree and a sentence is not. That is this row's ceiling argued from the other side, and it is also why the repair for the loud variant was to pick the member that says what the witness MEANS rather than to alias the old spelling: an alias would have made the import resolve while leaving the weaker predicate in place.",

    "HARM: an annotation that asserts the opposite of the code is worse than an absent one, because it is cited as coverage. The first specimen's doc told a reader that a control asserting `ScopeAccepted` establishes NOTHING about ambiguity -- on a branch where two negative controls do exactly that and are the wall's evidence. A reviewer who trusted the doc would have called the controls decorations; a reviewer who trusted the controls would have called the doc wrong. Both readings are available from one tree, which is the state DESIGN section 4b(1) calls rung inflation when it runs the other way.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. The defect is caught, when it is caught, by a person reading the merged text against the merged behaviour, which is review rather than a mechanism.",

    "CEILING: MITIGATABLE, AND THIS IS THE HONEST ANSWER RATHER THAN AN UNBUILT ONE. `this prose describes this code` is not a decidable property, so no wall can refuse a false annotation in general, and a trigger promising one would never fire -- DESIGN section 4b(2) calls that an untracked stall dressed as a plan. What IS decidable is narrower and is where the next rung sits.",

    "NEXT TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY AND SCOPED TO THE DECIDABLE SUBSET: a merge over annotated declarations that REFUSES to auto-resolve an annotation block whose ADJACENT DECLARATION differs between the two sides, requiring the merging author to state the resolution explicitly instead of taking a side. Adjacency and per-side difference are both decidable from the three trees, and either specimen above would have been refused by it: each doc block sits on a declaration the two sides changed differently. It does not decide truth and does not claim to -- it removes the case where the falsehood ships WITHOUT ANYONE BEING ASKED, which is every instance measured so far. Discriminating RED: a merge where one side edits a declaration and the other side edits its annotation, refused. Positive control: a merge where the annotation is untouched on both sides, or where only one side changed either, merged silently.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
